# Circular Seating Arrangement: Round Tables and Complex Puzzles

> Circular puzzles replace fixed seat numbers with neighbours, opposites,
> and clockwise counts. This lesson fixes the left-right convention around
> a table, breaks rotational symmetry, and walks an eight-person puzzle
> before adding hybrid facing, square tables, and floor-puzzle variants.

[important]
Memorise the convention once: facing the centre, a person's LEFT runs
anti-clockwise around the table and their RIGHT runs clockwise, as seen
from above. Facing outward reverses both. Every circular clue — "second to
the left", "third to the right" — is counted in the sitter's frame, not
the page's.
[/important]

## 1. The Circular Convention Box

Picture a clock face on the page. A person at the bottom of the circle,
facing the centre (facing up), has their left hand toward the page's
left — the 8 o'clock side: **anti-clockwise**.

```text
Facing the CENTRE:   left = anti-clockwise , right = clockwise
Facing OUTWARD:      left = clockwise      , right = anti-clockwise
```

**Counting "second to the left of X":** stand at X's seat facing the way
X faces, move one seat to your left, then one more — that second seat is
the answer. For centre-facing X, that is two seats anti-clockwise.

[tip]
Write a small arrow next to each person in your sketch: an L arrow for
anti-clockwise when centre-facing. When a clue says "immediate right,"
trace clockwise from that person — never from the page's perspective.
[/tip]

## 2. Breaking Rotational Symmetry

A circle has no seat 1 — any rotation of a valid arrangement is also
valid. So fix one person arbitrarily:

1. Place the person named in the most-used clue at any seat, say the top
   (12 o'clock) position.
2. Build the rest relative to them.
3. Answer questions relative to people, never to absolute seat numbers.

[definition]
**Rotational symmetry** means the circle can be spun without changing any
clue. Fixing one person's seat destroys this symmetry and turns the
circular puzzle into a fixed sequence of seats.
[/definition]

## 3. Worked Example: Eight Friends Around a Table

Eight friends A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H sit around a circular table, all
facing the centre.

```text
Clue 1: A sits second to the right of B.
Clue 2: C sits immediately to the left of A.
Clue 3: D sits opposite A.
Clue 4: F sits immediately to the left of B.
Clue 5: G sits second to the left of D.
Clue 6: E sits immediately to the left of D.
```

Step-by-step (number seats 1-8 clockwise, B fixed at seat 1):

```text
Clue 1: A second to right of B  -> clockwise 2 from seat 1 = seat 3
Clue 2: C immediately left of A -> anti-clockwise 1 from 3 = seat 2
Clue 3: D opposite A            -> 8 seats, opposite = 4 away = seat 7
Clue 4: F immediately left of B -> anti-clockwise 1 from 1 = seat 8
Clue 5: G second to left of D   -> anti-clockwise 2 from 7 = seat 5
Clue 6: E immediately left of D -> seat 6 ; H takes the last seat = 4
```

Final arrangement clockwise from B: **B, C, A, H, G, E, D, F**.

**Answers:** who sits opposite B? Seat 5 = G. Who are A's immediate
neighbours? C and H. Who sits third to the right of E? Clockwise from
seat 6: seat 7 (D), seat 8 (F), seat 1 (B) — so **B**.

## 4. Facing Outward and Hybrid Circles

When everyone faces **outward**, both directions flip: a person's left is
now clockwise. The clue "A sits second to the left of B" now counts two
seats clockwise from B.

**Hybrid puzzles** mix the two: some face the centre, some face outward.
The method:

1. Label each person with an inward or outward arrow as you read.
2. Convert every directional clue using that person's own facing.
3. Ignore facing for "opposite" — opposites are pure geometry.

In a hybrid, the same clue "immediate left" can mean anti-clockwise for
one person and clockwise for their neighbour, so never batch-convert.

[warning]
The most common circular error is mixing conventions mid-puzzle —
applying centre-facing rules to an outward-facing person. If your diagram
locks but one clue refuses to fit, re-check the facing of that clue's
person before redrawing.
[/warning]

## 5. Rectangular and Square Tables

At a square table with two people per side (eight total), most rules
carry over, but note:

| Feature | Circular table | Square table |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Neighbours | two (always adjacent) | two, except corner sitters have three |
| Opposite | the seat half-way around | the person directly across the table |
| "Second to the left" | two seats along the ring | counts people along the perimeter |

Corner seats belong to two sides; a clue like "P sits at a corner on the
north side" fixes both a corner and a side — stronger than any circular
clue, so use it as your symmetry-breaker, exactly like fixing the first
person in a circle.

## 6. Complex Add-Ons

**Floor puzzles** use the same engine with floors instead of seats:
eight people on four floors, two per floor. "A lives on floor 3" is
definite; "A lives above B" is relative; "C does not live on floor 1" is
negative — the definite-relative-negative order still rules.

**Adjacency constraints** import from linear puzzles: "exactly two
people sit between X and Y" or "X and Y are not neighbours" prune
possibilities after the main placements.

**Combi-puzzles** join two formats — a row and a circle sharing people,
or a double row feeding a circle. Solve the format with more definite
clues first, then carry the fixed facts into the second format.

## 7. Traps That Cost Marks

- **Clockwise/anti-clockwise swap:** reading "left" as clockwise because
  the page picture looks that way to you — trace the sitter's own frame.
- **Counting the starting seat:** "second to the right of X" means X's
  neighbour's neighbour, not X itself — off-by-one errors are endemic.
- **Odd-count opposites:** with an odd number of seats, nobody sits
  exactly opposite anyone; do not hunt for opposite pairs in a 7-seat
  circle.
- **Forgetting the wrap:** seat 8's clockwise neighbour is seat 1; the
  ring closes, and counts continue across the top of your numbering.

## 8. Strategy Summary

| Stage | Move |
| --- | --- |
| Read the stem | note the count, the facing (centre/out/hybrid) |
| Setup | draw the ring, fix the most-used person at the top |
| Phase 1 | place "opposite" clues first in even-count circles |
| Phase 2 | place immediate left/right clues, using each person's facing |
| Phase 3 | apply between-counts and negative constraints |
| Finish | verify every clue; answer relative questions, not seat numbers |

[example]
Exam method: **(1)** fix one person to kill rotational symmetry, **(2)**
convert every "left/right" clue through the sitter's own facing —
centre-facing means left is anti-clockwise, **(3)** use opposites early
in even circles, **(4)** close the ring when counting across seat 8,
**(5)** re-check every clue before answering.
[/example]
